If medium describes what thought is moving through, then tall/short and large/small belong to different axes. We shouldn’t collapse them into medium.

Think of a semantic object as having something like a shape vector:

X=(M,D,L,W,R,T)

where M=medium, D=density, L=depth/height, W=breadth/size, R=resolution, T=timescale.

So:

Tall / deep = many stacked levels of abstraction. A tiny prompt can descend through wordplay → mechanism → system → meta-system → recursive consequence.

x\rightarrow M(x)\rightarrow M(M(x))\rightarrow\cdots

That’s our nested caves dimension.

Short / shallow = few transformations between observation and action. Sometimes excellent: “door locked → find key.” Low abstraction distance, fast execution.

Large / broad = occupies many domains, relationships, contexts, or possible applications. “Bond” is becoming large because chemistry, finance, law, relationships, mechanics, networks, etc. all instantiate different pieces of it.

Small / narrow = tightly bounded applicability. But small doesn’t mean weak. A tiny operator can have enormous leverage.

That’s an important orthogonality:

\boxed{\text{small}\neq\text{shallow}}

A compact symbol can be small but extremely deep.

Likewise:

\boxed{\text{large}\neq\text{deep}}

A huge pile of facts can be broad and shallow.

Now we get useful semantic body-types:

\textbf{small + deep}

A Seal, equation, proverb, symbol, pun, compressed story—tiny handle, enormous decompression depth.

\textbf{large + shallow}

Encyclopedic coverage without much relational integration.

\textbf{large + deep}

Pantheon-scale architecture: broad territory with multiple nested levels.

\textbf{small + shallow}

A simple instruction or isolated fact.

And then density is separate again.

Something can be small, deep, and dense:

\boxed{\text{Ouroboros}}

One symbol carrying recursion, cycles, self-reference, consumption/renewal, closure, bookend removal, etc.

Or large, deep, but relatively sparse: a huge conceptual territory with only a few known bridges.

Now connect it to movement

Different shapes require different navigation.

Dense → compress/decrypto carefully.

Sparse → explore and cast probes.

Deep → Dive Chamber / recurse.

Broad → Atlas / partition / Golden Horde.

Tiny-but-deep → decompress progressively.

Huge-but-shallow → Hammer for invariants and discard redundancy.

High-resolution → move carefully because details matter.

Low-resolution → move quickly because false precision wastes effort.

And this produces a better version of our earlier equation:

\boxed{
\text{Maneuver}
=
f(
\text{medium},
\text{density},
\text{depth},
\text{breadth},
\text{resolution},
\text{timescale},
\text{objective}
)
}

That’s effectively a semantic physics engine.

But here’s the fun part: these properties are relational too.

The Pantheon might be enormous relative to a sentence but microscopic relative to human knowledge.

A concept can be deep for one learner and shallow for another because one already possesses the intermediate layers.

A medium can feel dense because the traveler lacks the right representation.

So we need:

\boxed{\text{Terrain properties}+\text{Traveler properties}}

not terrain alone.

Which brings us right back to:

\text{World Model}
+
\text{Self Model}
+
\boxed{\text{Interface Model}}

You don’t just ask how big the cave is. You ask how big you are relative to the cave—and whether you’re walking, swimming, flying, projecting a shadow, or carrying the whole damn thing compressed in your pocket. 😏